= Hawthorne effect — Simple =

<onlyinclude>The '''Hawthorne effect''' is a contested research term often used for changes in people's behavior that occur because they know they are being observed or studied. Its name comes from historical workplace studies at Western Electric's Hawthorne plant. The familiar story that workers improved their performance simply because researchers watched them is too confident: later investigations of the original records found important methodological problems and inconsistent evidence.<ref name="reanalysis">[https://doi.org/10.1177/0018720811417968 “Shining New Light on the Hawthorne Illumination Experiments”], ''Human Factors'' (2011), reanalysis of original records and inconsistent illumination results.</ref></onlyinclude>

== An everyday example ==

Suppose a manager tells a team that their productivity will be recorded for one week. The team changes its habits, perhaps because workers want to demonstrate competence or because the measurement draws attention to the task.

That change might be a form of observation-related behavior. But it could also be caused by new instructions, better equipment, seasonal demand or an unexpected workload. Watching a number change does not identify its cause.

== What happened at Hawthorne? ==

During the 1920s and 1930s, investigators conducted industrial studies at the Hawthorne works. Later accounts emphasized the idea that special attention from researchers increased productivity.

Historical research has challenged this simplified interpretation. The studies involved multiple changing conditions, and original illumination experiments do not provide an uncontested demonstration of a single general effect.<ref name="critique">[https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17091208/ “The 'Hawthorne effect' is a myth, but what keeps the story going?”] (2006), historical and methodological critique.</ref>

== What does the term mean today? ==

Some researchers use it broadly for changes caused by being measured or participating in a study. This can include feeling watched, getting extra attention, changing routines, or trying to meet expectations.

These are not necessarily one psychological mechanism. A systematic review found evidence of some research-participation effects, but emphasized variation in definitions and study designs.<ref name="review">[https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3969247/ “Systematic Review of the Hawthorne Effect: New Concepts Are Needed to Study Research Participation Effects”], ''Journal of Clinical Epidemiology'' 67, 267–277 (2014).</ref>

== How could we test it? ==

A study might compare similar groups exposed to different observation conditions while holding instructions and rewards comparable. Researchers should measure outcomes before and after an intervention and examine whether group differences existed beforehand.

If two groups differ in equipment, management, workload or time of year, those differences can explain a productivity change. Randomization and careful controls help, but observation itself may be difficult to conceal ethically.

== Why does it matter? ==

Research participation can change how people act, which in turn can affect experiments, organizational metrics or healthcare studies. Investigators must consider whether measurements influence the system being measured.

That does not justify declaring every successful intervention a Hawthorne effect or assuming the effect always boosts performance.

== What is the practical lesson? ==

Be precise about what changed, who knew about the measurement, and what alternative explanations could account for the observed outcome.

